1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,840
It's two in the morning.

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,680
A sixteen-year-old is sitting in his room,

3
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:05,420
staring at a number on his screen.

4
00:00:05,519 --> 00:00:07,459
Seventeen trillion, three hundred and

5
00:00:07,479 --> 00:00:09,599
thirty three billion rows of data.

6
00:00:09,739 --> 00:00:10,939
Inside Microsoft.

7
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:12,680
He wants to scream.

8
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:14,040
But his parents are asleep.

9
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:16,460
So he just sits there, and checks the math again.

10
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:18,341
A few hours earlier, he was doing his

11
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:19,501
Friday schoolwork.

12
00:00:19,521 --> 00:00:21,381
Now he has admin access to a system only

13
00:00:21,401 --> 00:00:23,501
Microsoft employees are supposed to see.

14
00:00:23,785 --> 00:00:25,565
And a few weeks later, Microsoft paid him

15
00:00:25,585 --> 00:00:26,845
five thousand dollars for it.

16
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:27,585
Was that fair?

17
00:00:27,605 --> 00:00:29,145
Stay till the end, and you decide.

18
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:30,301
I'm Vio.

19
00:00:30,381 --> 00:00:31,901
And at sixteen, I was struggling to

20
00:00:31,921 --> 00:00:34,021
remember my own Wi-Fi password.

21
00:00:34,141 --> 00:00:36,021
So this is the story of the kid who walked

22
00:00:36,041 --> 00:00:38,201
into Microsoft through the front door.

23
00:00:38,438 --> 00:00:40,118
Online, he goes by Faav.

24
00:00:40,138 --> 00:00:41,278
Sixteen years old.

25
00:00:41,298 --> 00:00:42,038
A student.

26
00:00:42,078 --> 00:00:43,238
And a bug hunter.

27
00:00:43,451 --> 00:00:45,011
His parents probably thought he was just

28
00:00:45,031 --> 00:00:46,231
gaming late at night.

29
00:00:46,251 --> 00:00:47,431
He was not gaming.

30
00:00:47,572 --> 00:00:48,812
Here's how that works.

31
00:00:48,832 --> 00:00:50,652
Big companies like Microsoft pay people to

32
00:00:50,672 --> 00:00:51,912
find holes in their systems,

33
00:00:51,932 --> 00:00:53,152
before criminals do.

34
00:00:53,172 --> 00:00:54,372
It's called a bug bounty.

35
00:00:54,392 --> 00:00:55,872
You find the problem, you report it,

36
00:00:55,892 --> 00:00:56,732
you get paid.

37
00:00:56,834 --> 00:00:58,394
But Faav didn't do it alone.

38
00:00:58,414 --> 00:01:00,514
He built his own AI hacking assistant.

39
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:01,854
He called it Antares.

40
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:04,017
Think of it like a partner that never sleeps.

41
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:06,077
It pokes at websites, reads the replies,

42
00:01:06,097 --> 00:01:07,817
and points at anything that looks weird.

43
00:01:07,927 --> 00:01:10,547
On August twenty fifth, Antares pointed at something.

44
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:12,187
A public doorway into a Microsoft system

45
00:01:12,207 --> 00:01:13,167
called Titan.

46
00:01:13,423 --> 00:01:15,163
Remember that number from the start.

47
00:01:15,183 --> 00:01:16,503
Five thousand dollars.

48
00:01:16,523 --> 00:01:17,903
We're coming back to it.

49
00:01:17,957 --> 00:01:20,477
Titan is an internal analytics platform.

50
00:01:20,517 --> 00:01:22,237
Basically, the place where Microsoft teams

51
00:01:22,257 --> 00:01:23,497
look at their data.

52
00:01:23,574 --> 00:01:25,714
Dashboards, charts, and the connections to

53
00:01:25,734 --> 00:01:27,210
some very, very big databases.

54
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:29,860
And it's meant to be employees only.

55
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:31,340
Picture an office building where every

56
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,200
door has a badge reader, and only staff

57
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:34,300
badges work.

58
00:01:34,387 --> 00:01:35,927
Faav didn't have a badge.

59
00:01:35,947 --> 00:01:37,627
But he had a hunch that the doors weren't

60
00:01:37,647 --> 00:01:39,047
checking badges properly.

61
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:40,323
The only problem?

62
00:01:40,383 --> 00:01:41,403
Proving it.

63
00:01:41,576 --> 00:01:44,276
For about ten days, everything he tried failed.

64
00:01:44,296 --> 00:01:44,876
Error.

65
00:01:44,896 --> 00:01:45,496
Error.

66
00:01:45,516 --> 00:01:46,496
Access denied.

67
00:01:46,743 --> 00:01:48,383
Most people would have given up.

68
00:01:48,403 --> 00:01:49,163
He had school.

69
00:01:49,183 --> 00:01:50,183
He had homework.

70
00:01:50,283 --> 00:01:52,503
And Microsoft is not exactly a small target.

71
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:54,402
Then, on Friday, September fourth,

72
00:01:54,422 --> 00:01:55,742
after finishing his schoolwork,

73
00:01:55,762 --> 00:01:57,322
he sat down and tried again.

74
00:01:57,484 --> 00:01:59,024
And sometime after one in the morning on

75
00:01:59,044 --> 00:02:00,624
Saturday, it worked.

76
00:02:00,773 --> 00:02:02,873
The system accepted him as an administrator.

77
00:02:03,193 --> 00:02:04,733
He could run his own database commands,

78
00:02:04,753 --> 00:02:05,953
as Titan's admin.

79
00:02:06,191 --> 00:02:08,231
And admin is not just any account.

80
00:02:08,251 --> 00:02:09,331
It's the master key.

81
00:02:09,351 --> 00:02:10,771
The account that can see everything,

82
00:02:10,791 --> 00:02:12,651
change settings, and run the show.

83
00:02:12,891 --> 00:02:14,131
His first command worked.

84
00:02:14,151 --> 00:02:14,871
Then another.

85
00:02:14,931 --> 00:02:16,171
Then he started counting.

86
00:02:16,413 --> 00:02:17,933
So what was the mistake?

87
00:02:18,153 --> 00:02:19,533
According to Faav, Titan looked at what

88
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:21,513
the digital ID said, but it never checked

89
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:23,213
whether the ID was real.

90
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:24,680
He described it perfectly.

91
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:26,420
It felt like a hotel where every door had

92
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:27,820
a working keycard reader,

93
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,900
but any keycard unlocked any room.

94
00:02:30,148 --> 00:02:32,028
So what was behind those doors?

95
00:02:32,248 --> 00:02:33,528
According to Faav's write-up,

96
00:02:33,548 --> 00:02:35,008
as reported by The Register,

97
00:02:35,068 --> 00:02:36,608
the platform's own records listed about

98
00:02:36,628 --> 00:02:39,148
twenty five thousand account and email entries.

99
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:42,163
Almost eighteen thousand employee email records.

100
00:02:42,303 --> 00:02:43,263
Job titles.

101
00:02:43,283 --> 00:02:44,123
Departments.

102
00:02:44,403 --> 00:02:45,903
Even who reports to whom.

103
00:02:46,035 --> 00:02:47,595
Faav pointed out that kind of information

104
00:02:47,615 --> 00:02:49,975
could be useful to scammers who trick employees.

105
00:02:50,315 --> 00:02:52,255
But he also said he never tested that.

106
00:02:52,316 --> 00:02:54,676
There were more than twenty four thousand dashboards.

107
00:02:54,816 --> 00:02:56,556
More than four hundred thousand charts.

108
00:02:56,848 --> 00:02:59,428
He even saw two tiny samples of Bing data,

109
00:02:59,448 --> 00:03:00,968
with search and location fields.

110
00:03:01,188 --> 00:03:03,308
But he said the locations were not precise.

111
00:03:03,488 --> 00:03:06,048
Not your exact address, just rough areas.

112
00:03:06,322 --> 00:03:08,342
And then, the number that kept him awake.

113
00:03:08,362 --> 00:03:10,962
Titan was connected to seventeen analytics databases.

114
00:03:10,982 --> 00:03:12,322
Adding up their sizes gave him about

115
00:03:12,342 --> 00:03:14,622
seventeen point three trillion rows.

116
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:16,930
Now, to be fair, that number is an

117
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:18,890
estimate from the system's own records.

118
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:21,570
It likely includes old and duplicated data.

119
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:23,950
And Faav didn't open or take those rows.

120
00:03:24,323 --> 00:03:25,822
But think about what that number means.

121
00:03:25,842 --> 00:03:27,793
A sixteen-year-old, from his bedroom,

122
00:03:27,813 --> 00:03:29,393
was standing at the door to one of the

123
00:03:29,413 --> 00:03:31,353
biggest data systems on the planet.

124
00:03:31,559 --> 00:03:33,659
So what did he do with that power?

125
00:03:33,849 --> 00:03:34,769
He reported it.

126
00:03:34,789 --> 00:03:36,649
That's the whole point of a bug bounty.

127
00:03:36,936 --> 00:03:38,576
And Microsoft moved fast.

128
00:03:38,596 --> 00:03:40,256
They asked him to stop testing.

129
00:03:40,296 --> 00:03:41,736
They even asked for his IP address,

130
00:03:41,756 --> 00:03:43,336
to confirm nothing bad had happened beyond

131
00:03:43,356 --> 00:03:44,476
his research.

132
00:03:44,668 --> 00:03:46,708
That might sound scary, but it's normal.

133
00:03:46,728 --> 00:03:48,328
A company has to be sure that the activity

134
00:03:48,348 --> 00:03:50,148
it saw came from the researcher,

135
00:03:50,168 --> 00:03:51,348
and nobody else.

136
00:03:51,599 --> 00:03:54,529
Within about a day, the doorway was locked down.

137
00:03:54,569 --> 00:03:56,329
Microsoft told him his report prompted an

138
00:03:56,349 --> 00:03:57,949
immediate investigation.

139
00:03:58,035 --> 00:03:59,095
And quick favour.

140
00:03:59,115 --> 00:04:00,575
If you like stories where one person takes

141
00:04:00,595 --> 00:04:02,235
on a giant, hit subscribe.

142
00:04:02,295 --> 00:04:03,735
A sixteen-year-old would appreciate it,

143
00:04:03,755 --> 00:04:04,795
and so would I.

144
00:04:04,947 --> 00:04:06,187
On September seventeenth,

145
00:04:06,207 --> 00:04:07,307
the reward arrived.

146
00:04:07,327 --> 00:04:08,727
Five thousand dollars.

147
00:04:08,879 --> 00:04:11,259
For a sixteen-year-old, that's a lot of money.

148
00:04:11,579 --> 00:04:12,719
But look at what he found.

149
00:04:12,879 --> 00:04:14,159
Admin access to an internal Microsoft

150
00:04:14,179 --> 00:04:17,239
platform, sitting on top of trillions of rows.

151
00:04:17,469 --> 00:04:19,489
One writer covering the story put it bluntly.

152
00:04:19,589 --> 00:04:21,609
They better have paid this kid a proper bounty.

153
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:23,333
But there is another side.

154
00:04:23,353 --> 00:04:25,478
Bug bounty programs usually pay from a

155
00:04:25,498 --> 00:04:26,091
fixed table.

156
00:04:26,111 --> 00:04:28,849
The price depends on the type of bug and

157
00:04:28,869 --> 00:04:30,381
on what was actually exposed,

158
00:04:30,401 --> 00:04:32,219
not on the biggest possible number.

159
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:33,751
And Microsoft's own programs advertise

160
00:04:33,771 --> 00:04:36,203
much bigger rewards for the most serious bugs.

161
00:04:36,223 --> 00:04:39,151
So in their eyes, this one may simply not

162
00:04:39,171 --> 00:04:40,431
have reached that level.

163
00:04:40,506 --> 00:04:41,786
And then there's the part that made people

164
00:04:41,806 --> 00:04:43,026
raise their eyebrows.

165
00:04:43,359 --> 00:04:45,148
Before his blog post went public,

166
00:04:45,168 --> 00:04:47,559
Faav says he rewrote it at Microsoft's request.

167
00:04:47,579 --> 00:04:48,464
Sections were cut.

168
00:04:48,484 --> 00:04:49,368
Numbers were removed.

169
00:04:49,388 --> 00:04:52,229
And the way he described the impact was changed.

170
00:04:52,401 --> 00:04:54,581
Microsoft's official statement thanked him.

171
00:04:54,601 --> 00:04:55,681
They said his report helped them better

172
00:04:55,701 --> 00:04:57,101
protect their customers, and that they

173
00:04:57,121 --> 00:04:59,181
look forward to working with Faav in the future.

174
00:04:59,273 --> 00:05:01,413
So on one side, a company that fixed the

175
00:05:01,433 --> 00:05:03,393
problem fast and paid the researcher.

176
00:05:03,613 --> 00:05:05,513
On the other, a teenager who found a door

177
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:07,733
into trillions of rows, got five thousand

178
00:05:07,753 --> 00:05:09,813
dollars, and had his story trimmed.

179
00:05:10,072 --> 00:05:11,732
And this isn't the first time a teenager

180
00:05:11,752 --> 00:05:12,852
beat a giant.

181
00:05:13,092 --> 00:05:15,052
In twenty nineteen, a fourteen-year-old in

182
00:05:15,072 --> 00:05:16,772
Arizona, Grant Thompson,

183
00:05:16,892 --> 00:05:18,632
found a FaceTime bug that let a caller

184
00:05:18,652 --> 00:05:20,252
hear the other person before they even

185
00:05:20,272 --> 00:05:21,165
picked up.

186
00:05:21,185 --> 00:05:23,765
His mom spent days trying to get Apple's attention.

187
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:25,965
Apple fixed it, thanked the family,

188
00:05:25,985 --> 00:05:27,825
and said it would help pay for his education.

189
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:30,041
So what's the lesson here?

190
00:05:30,129 --> 00:05:32,289
First, AI tools have changed who can find

191
00:05:32,309 --> 00:05:33,229
these holes.

192
00:05:33,729 --> 00:05:35,049
A teenager with a home-made assistant

193
00:05:35,069 --> 00:05:37,849
found something a trillion-dollar company missed.

194
00:05:38,066 --> 00:05:39,506
And that cuts both ways.

195
00:05:39,526 --> 00:05:40,826
If a kid can do it for a bounty,

196
00:05:40,846 --> 00:05:42,406
someone else could do it for a ransom.

197
00:05:42,663 --> 00:05:44,343
That's why security teams are now pointing

198
00:05:44,363 --> 00:05:46,543
AI at their own systems too.

199
00:05:47,083 --> 00:05:48,603
Whoever finds the hole first,

200
00:05:48,623 --> 00:05:49,183
wins.

201
00:05:49,536 --> 00:05:51,736
Second, if this kind of thing excites you,

202
00:05:51,756 --> 00:05:53,256
do it the way Faav did.

203
00:05:53,276 --> 00:05:54,876
Through official bug bounty programs,

204
00:05:54,896 --> 00:05:55,736
with permission.

205
00:05:55,796 --> 00:05:57,296
Testing a system without permission is a

206
00:05:57,316 --> 00:05:59,096
crime, even if you mean well.

207
00:05:59,153 --> 00:06:00,753
And Faav's own advice to every developer

208
00:06:00,773 --> 00:06:01,853
was simple.

209
00:06:02,233 --> 00:06:03,733
Always check that the ID is real,

210
00:06:03,753 --> 00:06:05,113
before anything else.

211
00:06:05,247 --> 00:06:06,887
So here's my question.

212
00:06:06,987 --> 00:06:09,047
Was five thousand dollars fair for what he found?

213
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:10,747
Or should Microsoft have paid him a lot more?

214
00:06:10,767 --> 00:06:11,827
Tell me in the comments.

215
00:06:12,041 --> 00:06:14,341
And subscribe, because next time,

216
00:06:14,361 --> 00:06:16,121
there's another story of one person who

217
00:06:16,141 --> 00:06:17,981
found a crack in something huge.

218
00:06:18,127 --> 00:06:20,267
At two in the morning, he couldn't scream.

219
00:06:20,347 --> 00:06:21,687
So he checked the math again.

220
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:23,227
And this time, the whole internet checked

221
00:06:23,247 --> 00:06:24,207
it with him.
